+# M6 plan · Non-platter mapping (2026-10-24)
+
+Goal (spec §5 M6, Michael's brief for run `rane-one-mixxx-2026-10-24`): every non-platter control of the MK1, as
+named in `reference/rane-one-mk1-controls.csv`, drives the right Mixxx 2.5.6 control through Components JS, with
+soft takeover on every knob and fader except the pitch fader (D27), handlers that never throw and **no output of
+any kind** (no `<outputs>`, no `midi.send*`, no component with a `midi` option). A census replayed through
+Midi Through into the dev Mixxx changes those controls, as its log shows. This plan only fixes the table, the
+structure, the harness and the check; M6-02..M6-09 build and verify, test-first.
+
+- **Decisions from this plan:** D30 *default* (pad modes), D31 *default* (deck buttons' Shift layers), D32
+  *default* (FX section), D33 *default* (library: browse encoder, Load, Back), D34 *default* (strip search guard).
+  Each cites the MK2 djay map as its hint and has a `To undo` line. Next free number after this plan: **D35** (M6-04 took it: channel faders start at 0).
+- **Mixxx names checked** in the Flatpak's 2.5.6 `share/mixxx/controllers/` and `skins/` (a one-off grep: every
+  control below is used by at least one shipped mapping or skin). Not found there, so not used: `[Mixer],…`
+  (2.5.6 still uses `[Master]`), `beatjump_size_halve`/`_double` (the script halves and doubles
+  `beatjump_size` itself), `[Library],ScrollVertical`, `hotcue_N_setloop`. M6-07's `--controller-abort-on-warning`
+  run is the proof for the rest: Mixxx warns on an unknown control, and that aborts.
+
+## Control table
+
+One row per row of `reference/rane-one-mk1-controls.csv` (137) except the platter pair (CC 6 and the pitch bend
+on channels 1 and 2: M7's). Generated by a one-off script from the CSV so no row is missed; deck 2's rows are deck
+1's with `[Channel2]`, `deck2` and the EffectUnit2 paddle. **Conf.** is the CSV's confidence; every `mk2` or
+`unknown` row is `UNVERIFIED` and its XML entry says so in its comment (M6-06), until round 2 step 9 or a photo
+of the panel settles the name.
+
+How to read it:
+- **Shift by note ("layered").** The One applies Shift itself: most deck buttons send a different note with Shift
+  held (census analysis § Census). Each such pair is **one** component with two notes (`note`, `shiftNote`); the
+  XML binds both notes to the same `input`, which picks the layer from the `control` number. It ignores the
+  containers' `shift()`.
+- **Shift by state ("same note").** The pitch bend buttons, the pad-mode buttons and Play send the same note either
+  way (Play's Shift note is UNVERIFIED: no census take has it). These components sit in each container's
+  `sameNote` sub-container, the only one `RaneOne.shift` calls `shift()`/`unshift()` on.
+- **Release from either layer.** A Shift-layer press may be released on the unshifted number when Shift is let go
+  first (03: `on ch1 18` released as `off ch1 7`). A layered component remembers which layer its press started
+  in; any release on either of its notes ends that press, and a release with no press is ignored. So a held
+  Shift layer (Shift+Reverse's `reverseroll`, a Shift+pad roll) always ends.
+- **Press only** = acts on the note-on (value > 0) and ignores the release; **momentary** = 1 on press, 0 on
+  release; **toggled** = flips the control on press.
+- **`no-op (counted)`** means `RaneOne.noop` or a store: the message is accepted (never unhandled), increments
+  `RaneOne.counts[<status ch number>]`, and changes no Mixxx control. Rows with no Mixxx function get it: Range,
+  Stop Time, Shift+Slip, the torque switch, the FX1–6 state report. Rows whose confidence is `unknown` but whose
+  function is clear enough to guess (trim, EQ, the cue buttons, the front-panel knobs) are mapped by that guess
+  and marked UNVERIFIED rather than dropped: an EQ that does nothing is worse at a gig than an EQ that might be
+  the wrong band, and round 2 step 9 checks the names.
+- **Soft takeover** is `engine.softTakeover(group, key, true)`, which Components' `Pot` calls from `connect()`
+  on its **first** input (`midi-components-0.0.js` 2.5.6, `Pot.input` → `connect`); M6-04's test sends a value
+  before it asserts the call.
